        - HIGH (applied), "uninformative" prior framing for Beta(1,1) is too strong
            - The chapter calls Beta(1,1) "flat" and uses it as the default. Flat on p is not invariant under reparameterization; on the log-odds scale it is heavy in the middle. Jeffreys prior for the binomial proportion is Beta(0.5, 0.5), which is invariant. Neither is "the" uninformative prior; the choice depends on what you want to be invariant under.
            - Edit: in Loop A, change "Beta(1, 1) prior is uniform on [0, 1]" to "Beta(1, 1) is uniform on the natural scale; Jeffreys prior is Beta(0.5, 0.5), which is uniform on the arcsine-transformed scale and invariant under reparameterization. Both are common 'default' choices; the right one depends on what you want to be invariant to. We use Beta(1, 1) throughout for transparency and to keep the exposition simple."
            - Defender: accept. Two sentences, no extra figures.
        - HIGH (applied), posterior-predictive numerical claims do not match the code
            - chapter.md says "The mode is around 12 (since true p ~ 0.6) but the spread covers 6 through 18."
            - Reproduced with bernoulli_sequence(50, p=0.6, seed=11) and the conjugate posterior predictive: heads observed = 33/50, posterior mean = 0.654, predictive mode = 13, 95% interval = [8, 18], 90% interval = [9, 17].
            - The story "if we had pretended p = 0.6 was known" is also strained because the posterior actually centres near 0.65, not 0.60, on this realization. The point about extra spread relative to a point estimate still holds but the numbers are off.
            - Edit: replace with "The mode is around 13 with 95% of the predictive mass between 8 and 18 heads. If we had plugged in the posterior-mean point estimate p_hat = 0.65 and used Binomial(20, 0.65), the 95% interval would be roughly [8, 17] -- visibly narrower because it ignores our remaining uncertainty about p."
            - Or, simpler, increase n in the demo from 50 to 200 so the posterior actually centres near 0.6 and the existing prose holds.
            - Defender: accept; either pick the "fix the numbers" path (one paragraph rewrite) or the "fix the demo" path (change one argument in generate.py and rerun). The latter is cleaner.
        - HIGH (applied), "credible interval" is not yet contrasted with frequentist coverage in this chapter
            - The carried-from-Chapter-5 line says "we have used credible interval intuitively for five chapters". But the chapter does not pin down the difference here: a 95% credible interval is "I assign 95% posterior mass to this interval given my prior and data", a 95% frequentist confidence interval is "across infinite repetitions of the experiment, the procedure traps the true p in 95% of intervals". They coincide numerically for Beta(1,1) priors with moderate-to-large N but mean different things.
            - Edit: in Loop A, after defining the posterior, add a short paragraph: "A 95% credible interval is 'I think p is in this range with 95% posterior probability'. A 95% confidence interval is 'this procedure, run on hypothetical replications, would trap the true p 95% of the time'. They give the same numbers in many simple problems, including this one with a flat prior, but the statements they license are different."
            - Defender: accept; one paragraph, ties the two-lens promise tighter.
